  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA low level ridge axis continues to slowly migrate east this \nmorning. Boundary layer decoupling and maintenance of weak low level \nflow has been sufficient for some vsby restrictions in fog \nespecially from west central Indiana into far northeast \nIndiana/northwest Ohio. Near term RAP/HRRR progs suggest \ngreatest potential for patchy dense fog early this morning \nroughly for southeast half of the area where low level flow will\nremain a bit weaker.\n\nAfter early morning fog dissipates. pleasant conditions are expected \ntoday with little in the way of thermal advection over the past 24 \nhours. With another day of similar full insolation, a \npersistence forecast from yesterday was utilized as a first \nguess max temp forecast for today with highs in the 50 to 55 \ndegree range.\n\nQuiet weather continues today/tonight with fog potential once \nagain the main concern tonight. Current thinking is that this \nwill continue to be a progressively lesser concern in comparison\nto the past few nights as low level flow will be a bit stronger\nwith slight eastward progression of the low level ridge axis. \nIf patchy shallow fog does develop again, would suspect that \nfar northeast Indiana into northwest Ohio would be more \nfavorable but forecast confidence is too low to include at this \ntime.\n\nWill be monitoring the progression of an upper level trough across \nthe Pac NW into California this morning as it lifts northeast to the \nUpper MS Valley by early Thursday. Low level height gradient will \nstrengthen downstream of this feature with sfc winds expected to \nincrease a bit each day for Wednesday and Thursday. Mixing heights \nin warm advection will be limited however, which should cap\ngusts to the 20 to 25 mph range Wed/Thu. No significant changes\nwere made to inherited max temps which may approach 60 in spots\non Wednesday and more firmly into the low to 60s for Thursday. \nThe aforementioned short wave will dampen the upper ridge, but \nnot to an extent that any notable forcing appears to extend as \nfar south as the local area. A dry forecast will be maintained \nthrough Thursday.\n\nLow level front associated with Great Lakes Thu wave will hang up \nwell north of the local area with southerly flow ramping up again \nFriday night into Saturday in advance of more substantial eastern \nPacific upper level trough. Medium range guidance hints at \npotential of two surges of better moisture advection, one well \nout ahead of the synoptic wave on Saturday evening, and then \naxis of better pooled moisture along accompanying low level \nfrontal zone for early Sunday morning. While this short wave \ndoes not have a full latitude connection to any better northern \nstream phasing, there does appear to be some better dynamics in \nplay with this system and decent frontal push which argues for \nmaintaining high chance/low likely rain shower PoPs Saturday \nnight into Sunday. This does not appear to be a significant \nsystem in terms of rainfall amounts however, as all forcing \nmechanisms and pockets of deeper moisture will be progressive in\nnature. Continued much above normal temperatures will continue \nFriday and Saturday transitioning to slightly below normal early\nnext week.\n\nLooking ahead, medium range guidance remains consistent in general \nidea that low level baroclinic zone will likely be sprawled \nacross the region for the early to middle portions of the next \nweek. Consensus idea is for maintenance of active eastern \nPacific parade of short waves, with some potential of episodes \nof stronger warm/moist advection over this pre-existing \nbaroclinic zone. This would likely result in some additional \nrain chances heading into the Day 8 to 10 timeframe.",
